    "proof_tex": "On \\(\\kappa_n=0\\), every point of \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) has coordinate magnitude at most \\(T_n^{\\mathrm{enc}}+1\\).  The coarse construction (1)--(7) in the deterministic-certificate proof bounds the center, half-width, donor allowance, and branch hull by a fixed polynomial in\n\\[\nn+T_n^{\\mathrm{enc}}+\\alpha^{-1}+1.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe fallback radius \\(M_0=B^2/c\\) is fixed.  The handle may choose every recorded radius no larger than the diameter of that coarse hull plus one outward rounding unit.  Increasing fixed constants once proves the first inequality.\n\nNow impose the three stable spectral inequalities.  With \\(\\tau=(cn/2)^2\\), the retained and discarded squared singular values are separated from \\(\\tau\\) by at least\n\\[\n\\min\\left\\{(3cn/4)^2-(cn/2)^2,\n(cn/2)^2-(cn/4)^2\\right\\}\n={3c^2n^2\\over16}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus the stable-box part of the certified-error-recurrence lemma applies.  It proves, from the explicit matrix-sign, residual-inversion, product, scalar, quantile, wrapping, and branching recurrences, that the sharp branch is certified and that each endpoint radius is at most\n\\[\nK_\\star(n+T_n^{\\mathrm{enc}}+\\alpha^{-1}+1)^{k_\\star}\n\\bigl(2^{-p_n}+2^{1-q_n}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe lower bound \\(s_r(X')\\ge3cn/4\\) justifies every inversion; the discarded upper bound certifies the rank split; and the largest-singular-value bound controls every intermediate magnitude.  No individual singular vector is selected, so repeated retained singular values are allowed.  Exact-dyadic public controls contribute zero initial radius.\n\nBy construction, \\(\\Delta_n^{\\mathrm{enc}}\\) is the sum of the endpoint radii attributable to the coordinate boxes and \\(\\Delta_n^{\\mathrm{arith}}\\) is the sum attributable to directed arithmetic and certified branching.  The recurrence has only a fixed number of endpoint stages and \\(O(\\log(q_n+2))\\) sign stages; its stable proof absorbs their polynomial count into \\(K_\\star,k_\\star\\).  Hence their sum satisfies (3).  Every recorded summand is nonnegative and no larger than the sum, which proves the last assertion.",
